ECT, ARRAY types support path notation: `src:customer.name::STRING`, `src:price::NUMBER(10,2)`. Always cast to usable types.\n- **VARIANT null vs SQL NULL**: JSON `null` is stored as `\"null\"`. Use `STRIP_NULL_VALUE = TRUE` on load to treat JSON nulls as SQL NULLs.\n- **Micro-partition pruning**: Queries automatically prune irrelevant micro-partitions based on filter predicates.\n- **Time Travel**: Access historical data (0\u201390 days retention) using `AT` or `TIMESTAMP` clauses without needing backups.\n- **Zero-copy cloning**: Clone databases/tables without additional storage cost.\n\n---\n\n## Common Development Workflows\n\n### Data Ingestion Pipeline\n1. **Extract** \u2014 Pull data from source systems using Snowpipe, Snowpipe Streaming, Openflow connectors, or third-party ETL tools (Fivetran, Informatica, etc.)\n2. **Stage** \u2014 Land raw data in a staging area (LND or RAW schema) using internal/external stages\n3. **Transform** \u2014 Clean, conform, and model data through:\n   - SQL-based transformations (Streams + Tasks)\n   - Dynamic Tables\n   - dbt models\n   - Snowpark for complex logic\n4. **Curate** \u2014 Build dimensional models (star/snowflake schema) in INT/DMRT schemas\n5. **Consume** \u2014 Expose curated data via secure views, semantic views, or external BI tools\n\n### Typical Development Lifecycle\n- **Development** (DEV account/warehouse) \u2192 **Testing** (TEST/UAT) \u2192 **Production** (PROD)\n- Use **Git** for version control of SQL scripts and DDL\n- Use **CI/CD** pipelines to deploy schema changes across environments\n- **Monitoring**: Query profiling, warehouse usage tracking, cost monitoring via `ACCOUNT_USAGE` views\n\n---\n\n## Typical User Roles\n\n### Data Engineers\n- **Responsibilities**: Build and maintain data pipelines (ingestion, transformation, distribution); manage virtual warehouses; optimize query performance; implement CDC with Streams; create and manage tables, schemas, databases; set up Snowpipe ingestion; manage data quality\n- **Tools**: Snowpipe, Streams, Tasks, Dynamic Tables, Snowpark, dbt, Snowflake CLI\n- **Skills**: SQL proficiency, Python/Java/Scala (Snowpark), ETL/ELT frameworks, data modeling, performance tuning\n\n### Data Analysts\n- **Responsibilities**: Write analytical SQL queries; create secure views; build and consume BI dashboards; validate business metrics; collaborate with product and business teams\n- **Tools**: Snowsight (web UI), BI tools (Power BI, Tableau, Looker), SQL\n- **Skills**: Advanced SQL (window functions, CTEs, aggregates), data interpretation, business acumen\n\n### Snowflake Developers\n- **Responsibilities**: Design and implement data warehouse solutions; write and optimize SQL queries and stored procedures; manage data migrations; configure virtual warehouses, data sharing, and zero-copy cloning; implement security measures (encryption, RBAC, data masking); create and manage data pipelines\n- **Skills**: SQL, data modeling, Snowpark, dbt, performance tuning, security implementation, CI/CD\n\n### Additional Roles\n- **BI Developers**: Create reports, dashboards, and visualizations\n- **Data Scientists**: Explore datasets, run feature engineering, use Snowpark for Python, access training data\n- **DevOps / Cloud Engineers**: Monitor warehouse usage, automate deployments, manage cost controls, set up integrations\n- **Security & Governance Teams**: Implement RBAC, encryption, access controls, masking policies, row access policies, network policies\n- **Database Administrators (DBAs)**: Manage Snowflake environment health, performance, security, backup policies\n\n---\n\n## Snowflake SQL Best Practices\n\n### Naming & Style\n1. **Use snake_case** for all identifiers (tables, columns, CTEs, views). Avoid double-quoted identifiers \u2014 they create case-sensitive names requiring constant quoting.\n2. **Use UPPERCASE for SQL keywords** and **lowercase/snake_case for object names**:\n   ```sql\n   SELECT user_id, event_type FROM prod_db.analytics.events;\n   ```\n3. **Always use fully qualified names** (`database.schema.object`) \u2014 never rely on `USE DATABASE`/`USE SCHEMA` context:\n   ```sql\n   SELECT * FROM prod_db.analytics.daily_metrics;\n   ```\n4. **Use CTEs (WITH clauses)** for complex queries involving 3+ tables instead of nested subqueries.\n5. **Use `CREATE OR REPLACE`** for idempotent DDL.\n6. **Never use `SELECT *` in production** \u2014 Snowflake's columnar storage scans only referenced columns, so explicit column lists reduce I/O and prevent schema coupling.\n7. **Use singular terms** for tables and fields (e.g., `Customer` table with `customer_id` field).\n8. **Aim for brief but meaningful names** \u2014 Snowflake allows up to 128 characters, but try to stay under 24. Use comments or tags for longer descriptions.\n9. **Avoid reserved keywords** as identifiers (e.g., `WHERE`, `VIEW`, `DATE`, `ORDER`, `USER`).\n10. **Avoid special characters** (except `_` and `$`). Stick to letters, numbers, underscores, and dollar signs.\n11. **Avoid mixed case** \u2014 Snowflake converts unquoted identifiers to uppercase, making mixed-case names require constant quoting.\n\n### Query Writing\n- Use **CTEs** for readability and reusability of complex multi-step transformations\n- Use **explicit column lists** in `INSERT` statements\n- Use **`IF NOT EXISTS`** or **`CREATE OR REPLACE`** for idempotent object creation\n- Use **`LISTAGG`** for string aggregation (not `WM_CONCAT` or other dialect-specific functions)\n- Use **`DATEADD`**, **`DATEDIFF`**, **`DATE_TRUNC`** for date operations (not `DATE_SUB`, `TIMESTAMPDIFF`, etc.)\n- For VARIANT data, **always cast** to usable types: `raw_data:user_id::STRING`\n\n### Data Modeling\n- Use **surrogate keys** (Snowflake sequences) instead of natural keys for primary keys\n- Use **foreign keys** to reference other tables\n- Use **consistent prefixes** for related columns (e.g., `address_street`, `address_city`)\n- Follow **Kimball dimensional modeling** where